WEBVTT

00:00:00.642 --> 00:00:22.002
<v Casey>Hey there — today we've got a nucleus shaped like a bowling pin from the Large Hadron Collider, a mosquito study that finally explains why some people get eaten alive and others don't, and a five-hundred-year-old shipwreck sitting deeper than most submarines can go. Plus a Deep Dive on whether patient science offers a different kind of purpose than tech ever did. Coming right up.

00:00:37.977 --> 00:00:56.937
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Casey. It's Sunday, August twenty-third, twenty twenty-six. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World.

00:00:57.937 --> 00:01:13.161
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We've got a Large Hadron Collider discovery, a mosquito mystery that finally has an answer, a five-hundred-year-old shipwreck, and a fair bit in between. Good day for anyone who likes staring at the world and asking why it works the way it does.

00:01:13.953 --> 00:01:42.129
<v Casey>Quick weather sweep before we get into any of it. Horsefly Lake is sitting at fourteen degrees with slight showers this morning, high of twenty-two, low of thirteen tonight, and tomorrow's shaping up as light drizzle with around twelve millimetres coming down over the day. Around the rest of the region — 100 Mile House is at twelve with light drizzle, Williams Lake is fifteen and also drizzly, Quesnel's at sixteen under overcast skies, and out on the Chilcotin plateau, Tatla Lake is sitting at twelve, overcast.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.954
<v Riley>Damp pretty much everywhere. Bring a jacket.

00:01:45.154 --> 00:01:46.178
<v Casey>Let's get into the news.

00:01:47.578 --> 00:02:20.746
<v Casey>Lighter day on the local front, heavier on the science wire, so we'll move through the Cariboo stories at a decent clip. Start with 100 Mile House, where RCMP are looking into a single-vehicle crash that turned out to be stranger than the initial call suggested. Sergeant Brian Lamb says a witness saw a grey Chevrolet pickup drive onto a property on Horse Lake Road, near Sven Road, back on Wednesday, August 12th. The witness watched a woman get out of the truck, cut the property's fence, then drive onto the field — before the vehicle ended up in the crash that brought police out in the first place.

00:02:21.895 --> 00:02:27.847
<v Riley>So the fence-cutting came before the crash, not after. Strange sequence for a single-vehicle call.

00:02:29.008 --> 00:02:36.456
<v Casey>It is, and the RCMP haven't released a motive or charges yet. A couple of fences took the physical brunt of it — cheap ending, strange middle.

00:02:37.751 --> 00:03:07.063
<v Riley>From there to Victoria, where the provincial Conservative caucus is showing real strain. The party's president has resigned, and the Tribune's reporting describes ongoing division inside the caucus that's been building since Kerry-Lynne Findlay became leader on May 30th. No public resignation letter has surfaced with specifics, just the fact of the departure and reporting that Findlay has been installing loyalists to key positions in both the caucus and the party apparatus.

00:03:08.479 --> 00:03:28.615
<v Casey>In the Okanagan, that same kind of political friction is playing out at the constituency level. A small group of protesters turned out to raise awareness of a formal campaign to recall MLA Tara Armstrong. A recall campaign in B.C. has to clear a specific signature threshold within the riding to force a by-election, so it's a real mechanism, even if it's an uphill one.

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:38.120
<v Riley>Those campaigns almost never clear the bar historically, but the attempt itself tells you something about how contested that seat is.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:04:03.880
<v Casey>Then the story with the most direct bearing on daily life around here. Poor air quality settled over the Southern Interior as wildfires continued burning across the province. The warning reported in the 100 Mile Free Press suggested those conditions could persist for another day or two beyond publication. If you're in a smoke-prone valley — and a lot of the Cariboo qualifies — check current conditions before you plan anything outdoors.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:28.624
<v Riley>From smoke in the air to something more mundane and, honestly, satisfying. A peer-reviewed study built from Backblaze's own hard drive records — researchers looked at 1.66 million drive-years across 443,000 individual hard drives and found HGST drives came out most reliable overall, while Seagate and Toshiba units failed at roughly twice the rate of Western Digital and HGST.

00:04:29.193 --> 00:04:33.305
<v Casey>A number that changes a purchasing decision, not just a press release headline.

00:04:34.571 --> 00:04:53.187
<v Riley>And it's a rare case of a company generating real longitudinal science just by running its own business for over a decade and keeping honest records. Nobody applied for a grant to figure out which drive brand lasts longest. They tracked failures as they happened and eventually somebody crunched the numbers.

00:04:54.087 --> 00:05:28.135
<v Casey>Two different fire research efforts are working the same underlying problem from opposite ends. NASA is flying research planes into pyrocumulonimbus clouds this summer — fire-driven thunderstorms that loft smoke and ash into the stratosphere — collecting samples from inside the plumes to understand how they form and what they carry. Meanwhile, a restoration project in Portugal, led by an organization called EcoAtivo, is trying to prevent fires from getting that intense in the first place, rewetting old wetlands and rebuilding small dams so water breaks up the landscape enough to interrupt how fire spreads.

00:05:29.588 --> 00:05:39.140
<v Riley>Two very different tools aimed at the same enemy. One's flying into the smoke after the fact, the other's trying to make sure there's less of it to fly into.

00:05:40.306 --> 00:06:03.970
<v Casey>And in Summerland, the wildfire crisis pulled in a somewhat unlikely group of allies. The B.C. wine industry has been stepping up to support growers and residents affected by the Bald Range fire — Paul Sawler of Dirty Laundry Winery said it "kind of restores your faith in human nature." Small mention in the bigger fire season story, but it's the kind of mutual aid that never shows up in official damage assessments.

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:24.794
<v Riley>On the security side, TechCrunch reported that someone posing as staff from a cryptocurrency news outlet used a fake conference invitation and Google Docs to try to deliver malware to a group of cybersecurity researchers. The lure was convincing enough that several professionals in the field engaged with it before recognizing the scam.

00:06:25.324 --> 00:06:27.236
<v Casey>Cheap trick, expensive if it lands.

00:06:28.459 --> 00:06:57.323
<v Riley>New Atlas flagged a physics story too — researchers have demonstrated something called "light pumping," bending beams of light on femtosecond timescales, a step toward computing systems that move data with light instead of electrical signals. Early-stage lab work, but this is the kind of materials science that shows up inside consumer hardware a decade later, the way earlier fiber-optic research eventually became the internet backbone running through places like the Cariboo today.

00:06:58.223 --> 00:07:19.439
<v Casey>Bigger picture from Scientific American — physicists say they're closer to explaining why the universe is made of matter instead of equal parts matter and antimatter, which by the physics we understand should have annihilated each other at the Big Bang. Researchers have pinned down a quantity called baryon number that may account for the tiny mismatch that let matter win out.

00:07:20.785 --> 00:07:27.865
<v Riley>That mismatch is the reason any of this exists — the planet, us, this show. A small number with an enormous consequence.

00:07:28.996 --> 00:07:44.364
<v Casey>(deadpan) On a lighter note, Hackaday has a piece on using steam to peel fruit — a clever bit of kitchen engineering that also works as an entry-level lesson in thermodynamics for anyone building heat-cycle projects at home. Thermodynamics shows up in your kitchen as much as in a collider.

00:07:45.544 --> 00:08:02.952
<v Riley>ScienceAlert reported new U.S. cardiology guidelines on managing cholesterol — updated thresholds and treatment recommendations from the American cardiology bodies. More a clinical update than a discovery, but good to know if it's part of your own health routine or a family member's.

00:08:03.852 --> 00:08:20.388
<v Casey>Popular Mechanics has the find of the week, honestly. Navy researchers scanning the seafloor off the coast of France picked up a sonar ping and traced it down to a five-hundred-year-old shipwreck sitting at eighty-two hundred feet — the deepest wreck ever documented along that stretch of coastline.

00:08:21.524 --> 00:08:32.548
<v Riley>Eighty-two hundred feet is deeper than most crewed submarines are rated to dive. Remote-operated vehicle territory, which means whatever's down there has been untouched for five centuries.

00:08:33.976 --> 00:08:49.640
<v Casey>Last one before the break. ScienceAlert reports that researchers think ancient humans may have evolved a specific physiological adaptation to survive the last Ice Age — the article doesn't spell out the full mechanism yet, so we'll leave the details until there's a clearer paper to point to.

00:08:50.927 --> 00:09:01.903
<v Riley>Decent bridge into where we're headed next, because today's Deep Dive is built around this kind of science — patient, strange, sometimes centuries in the making before anyone notices the payoff.

00:09:02.703 --> 00:09:06.879
<v Casey>Before we get there, a quick word about who's doing that patient work close to home.

00:09:08.279 --> 00:09:15.439
<v Riley>Quick meta moment before we move on — we actually looked back at last week's episode and tightened up a few things we were doing wrong.

00:09:15.619 --> 00:09:16.323
<v Casey>Define "wrong."

00:09:16.969 --> 00:09:31.105
<v Riley>Fair. I mean we noticed I was front-loading the data dumps. Like, throwing numbers at people before they knew why they should care. So we restructured how I intro the tech stories — lead with the human problem first, *then* the numbers that prove it matters.

00:09:31.659 --> 00:09:40.067
<v Casey>So listeners won't have to rewind when Riley drops a statistic about broadband penetration in rural BC and they're thinking, "Wait, why does this matter to me?"

00:09:40.387 --> 00:09:59.691
<v Riley>Exactly. Also — and I know you're going to appreciate this — we flagged places where I was doing the whole "tech will fix this" move without actually naming who's going to maintain it. So we added more accountability language. Less "innovation solves everything" and more "here's who's responsible if this actually works."

00:10:00.010 --> 00:10:01.386
<v Casey>Look at that. You're learning.

00:10:02.044 --> 00:10:07.340
<v Riley>Don't sound so surprised. Anyway, hopefully you'll hear the difference this week. Ready to dig in?

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:40.396
<v Casey>(warmly) The Scout Island Nature Centre in Williams Lake is volunteer-run through the Williams Lake Field Naturalists, and it's the kind of quiet infrastructure that never makes headlines but keeps running year after year — birding walks, Salmon in the Classroom, interpretive trails around the marsh. Right now they're running kids' summer programs with a different theme every week: Water one week, Bugs the next, Birds after that. If you've got a kid who asks too many questions about what's crawling under a log by the shoreline, that's the place to send them. Sign-up is at scoutisland.ca.

00:10:40.934 --> 00:10:50.422
<v Riley>A real specimen-collection pipeline hiding in plain sight. Kids counting bugs at Scout Island this summer, some of them counting them professionally in twenty years.

00:10:51.222 --> 00:10:53.518
<v Casey>Which is a decent way into today's Deep Dive.

00:10:54.918 --> 00:11:13.206
<v Riley>So CERN just found out that some atomic nuclei aren't round. Physicists at the Large Hadron Collider collided neon-20 nuclei and found evidence they're shaped more like a bowling pin — an oxygen-16 core with an extra alpha cluster, a helium-4 nucleus, stuck onto one end, breaking the expected symmetry.

00:11:13.490 --> 00:11:16.530
<v Casey>A bowling pin. That's the actual term physicists are using?

00:11:16.792 --> 00:11:43.600
<v Riley>That's the metaphor everyone's running with. But the reason it matters isn't the shape by itself. Nuclear geometry determines how particles spray outward when you smash nuclei together at high energy, and that collision technique is how physicists recreate the ultra-hot, ultra-dense quark-gluon plasma believed to have filled the universe microseconds after the Big Bang. Get the shape wrong, and your model of that primordial soup is off.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:56.128
<v Casey>Pear-shaped nuclei in radium and radon were reported years back, though. This isn't a brand-new phenomenon appearing out of nowhere. It's a refinement of something nuclear physicists have suspected and modeled for a while.

00:11:56.779 --> 00:12:13.779
<v Riley>Calling it incremental undersells what a decades-long collider program is built to do. Nobody constructs the LHC to get one headline. You build it to keep sharpening the picture, year after year, until the model is accurate enough that the physics built on top of it can hold weight.

00:12:14.402 --> 00:12:29.394
<v Casey>My actual question is whether the bowling pin is doing scientific work or communications work. "Atomic nucleus shaped like a bowling pin" is a far better headline than "refinement of alpha-clustering models in light nuclei." Somebody chose that framing deliberately.

00:12:30.024 --> 00:12:44.224
<v Riley>Probably both things are true at once. Translating dense physics into an image a person can hold in their head isn't a trick — it's how curiosity spreads past the people who read the paper. Most listeners are never opening that journal.

00:12:44.768 --> 00:12:50.920
<v Casey>And that's fine as long as the communication doesn't outrun the finding. When it does, you get disappointment later.

00:12:51.434 --> 00:13:01.498
<v Riley>Agreed. But in this case, the University of Copenhagen team and the CERN collaborators are saying the data supports the bowling-pin geometry. The metaphor tracks the result.

00:13:01.998 --> 00:13:21.398
<v Casey>So what's the question underneath all of this? A lot of people in the knowledge economy right now — tech especially — are struggling to explain why their work matters. Does a project like the LHC, or a mosquito study running for years in a university lab, offer a different relationship to purpose? Or does that fall apart the same way once funding shifts?

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:52.636
<v Riley>The mosquito research is a decent test case. The Vosshall lab at Rockefeller University found that people carrying higher levels of carboxylic acids in their skin secretions — produced by skin bacteria — are significantly more attractive to mosquitoes, and that "mosquito magnet" status holds steady year over year. Published in Cell, NIH-funded, solid backing. Separately, the Cardé lab at UC Riverside found mosquitoes will land on a scent-treated target based on odor combinations alone — no heat, moisture, or visual cues needed.

00:13:53.299 --> 00:13:58.355
<v Casey>Two independent labs, different angles, same underlying mechanism. That's actual replication.

00:13:58.876 --> 00:14:15.532
<v Riley>And it validates something people have said anecdotally for as long as mosquitoes have existed — "I really am the one they go for" — with real chemistry underneath it. Wonder showing up in something completely ordinary. Not a black hole collision. A backyard mosquito at Horsefly Lake in July.

00:14:16.177 --> 00:14:33.929
<v Casey>(wry) Except there's no product at the end of it yet. No skin-chemistry-based repellent you can buy. The elegant lab finding and the thing that helps you on your porch this evening are still two separate objects, and a lot of the popular coverage blurs that gap when it runs headlines like "science solves why mosquitoes bite you."

00:14:34.523 --> 00:14:52.803
<v Riley>The Nautilus piece actually complicates it further — different mosquito species respond to different chemical profiles, so you might be a magnet for Aedes aegypti but not for the species biting you in the Cariboo. "Universal mosquito magnet" turns out to be an oversimplification even of the real finding.

00:14:53.348 --> 00:15:02.500
<v Casey>Which strengthens my point. The underlying biology is still being worked out, and the popular coverage keeps rounding up to a cleaner story than the data supports.

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:28.588
<v Riley>But that doesn't cancel the discovery. It means the mechanism is richer than one headline. The gap between "we understand the mechanism" and "here's a spray that works" is real, but the research still moved. The Rockefeller team identified carboxylic acids as the key variable, the UC Riverside team showed scent alone is sufficient — those are durable findings that hold whether or not a product ships this fiscal year.

00:15:28.488 --> 00:15:53.256
<v Casey>Nobody said the findings aren't durable. The question is whether durability of discovery translates into durability of purpose for the people doing the work. NIH funding gets contested every political cycle. CERN's budget gets renegotiated by member states with their own domestic pressures. The idea that patient science has a fundamentally different relationship to meaning assumes stable institutional backing that isn't guaranteed anywhere.

00:15:53.926 --> 00:16:04.566
<v Riley>It's more stable than a startup's eighteen-month runway, though. A ten-year physics program has survivability built into its design in a way a venture-backed company structurally can't match.

00:16:05.224 --> 00:16:15.040
<v Casey>Different failure mode. Ask anyone who watched a long-running observatory get its funding pulled mid-decade because a budget committee decided the science wasn't glamorous enough that year.

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:29.740
<v Riley>(thoughtful) Even inside that fragility, though, the people doing the work don't describe their careers the way tech workers increasingly do. Nobody at Rockefeller is publicly wondering online whether mosquito research was a waste of a decade.

00:16:30.299 --> 00:16:49.955
<v Casey>Maybe because the output has an object that exists outside the company that funded it. A tech worker's code often lives only inside somebody else's platform — it gets deprecated, the company gets acquired, the product gets sunset, and years of work disappear along with the org chart. A nucleus doesn't stop being bowling-pin-shaped because a board voted to pivot.

00:16:50.562 --> 00:16:59.802
<v Riley>(chuckles) The durability of the object produced, not just the durability of the funding around it. A finding survives even when the institution that produced it doesn't.

00:17:00.202 --> 00:17:01.274
<v Casey>Okay. That one lands.

00:17:01.374 --> 00:17:17.846
<v Casey>Don't get used to it. The maintenance question's still open — who keeps a long baseline running once the initial excitement wears off and the funding committee moves its attention to the next proposal? Big science has that problem at a bigger scale, it just has deeper reserves to absorb the gaps.

00:17:18.546 --> 00:17:44.442
<v Riley>Which is where Scout Island matters — not as a nice local aside, but as a working answer to that continuity problem. A kid doing Bugs week this summer isn't publishing in a journal. But that program has run for years through volunteer continuity inside the Williams Lake Field Naturalists. It's not dependent on one grant cycle, one champion staff member, or one funding announcement surviving a change in government.

00:17:44.984 --> 00:17:58.504
<v Casey>It's small enough that burnout doesn't sink the whole thing. If one volunteer steps back, the program doesn't collapse, because nobody built their entire professional identity around it being the mission of their life. The ceiling's lower, but so is the fall.

00:17:59.017 --> 00:18:27.953
<v Riley>And maybe that's the contrast worth drawing with the tech story. The industry sold itself as mission-driven for two decades when it was venture-driven the whole time, and when the venture math stopped working, the mission story collapsed along with it, taking a lot of people's sense of purpose down too. A nature centre never oversold itself as changing the world. It just kept teaching kids to identify beetles, year after year, without a founder's manifesto attached.

00:18:28.285 --> 00:18:29.813
<v Casey>Lower stakes, lower collapse.

00:18:30.499 --> 00:18:37.739
<v Riley>Not glamorous. But honest about what it is, which might count for more than a mission statement nobody in the building believed.

00:18:38.339 --> 00:18:44.619
<v Casey>So where does that leave somebody listening who isn't going to work at CERN or study mosquito chemistry at Rockefeller?

00:18:44.949 --> 00:19:03.125
<v Riley>They can put weight behind the version of that instinct that already exists here. A Bugs week at Scout Island, a Salmon in the Classroom cohort, a December bird count out past Horsefly — Cariboo-scale versions of the same curiosity that eventually builds an LHC. No quarterly earnings call attached.

00:19:03.763 --> 00:19:21.355
<v Casey>The structural question worth chasing, if anyone wants to take it further: could a regional institution like UNBC formally partner with a group like the Field Naturalists to give amateur observation an analytical home that outlasts any one summer program? Instead of the data resetting every June when the season ends.

00:19:21.927 --> 00:19:47.775
<v Riley>That's the piece to investigate. Whether the observations those kids and volunteers generate over years ever get structured well enough that a researcher could build a real ecological baseline from them, instead of the data sitting in a filing cabinet at the nature centre. If you're in the Cariboo and have thoughts on that — or a story tip, or a correction on something we got wrong — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:19:48.406 --> 00:19:58.622
<v Casey>And if you're annoyed we didn't land firmly on one side of the tech question — some days the honest answer is we don't know yet. The science keeps moving. The meaning part is yours to sort out.

00:19:58.921 --> 00:20:00.265
<v Riley>Have a good rest of your Sunday.

00:20:00.624 --> 00:20:04.104
<v Casey>Go swat a mosquito. You'll appreciate the research more afterward.